## Practice Exercise: Pseudoscorpion Mating

### 1. Observed means

Females in the Double treatment averaged 3.625 successful broods.
Females in the Single treatment averaged 2.200 successful broods.

The Double treatment had 1.425 more successful broods on average.

### 2. Randomization function

My randomize_broods() function uses sample() without replacement to
shuffle Successful_broods while keeping treatment assignments fixed.

### 3. Randomized difference

For each shuffled dataset, I calculated the mean for the Double
treatment minus the mean for the Single treatment.

### 4. Repeated randomizations

I repeated the randomization 10,000 times and stored the differences
in random_differences.

### 5. Extreme results

There were 234 randomizations with a difference at least as large
in absolute magnitude as the observed difference of 1.425.

### 6. p-value and conclusion

p = 234 / 10,000 = 0.0234.

At a significance level of 0.05, I reject the null hypothesis of no
difference between treatments. The higher observed mean in the Double
treatment supports the idea that mating with two different males
increases successful brood production.

## Final Problem 1: Are Most Toads Right-Handed?

### 1. Individual handedness

I used 100,000 simulations per toad under the null hypothesis that
each hand had a probability of 0.5. I classified significant results
using a two-sided p-value below 0.05.

| Toad | Sex | Trials | Right uses | p-value | Conclusion |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| T1 | F | 16 | 14 | 0.00376 | Right-handed |
| T2 | M | 15 | 12 | 0.03520 | Right-handed |
| T3 | M | 17 | 3 | 0.01264 | Left-handed |
| T4 | M | 15 | 15 | 0.00008 | Right-handed |
| T5 | M | 16 | 12 | 0.07644 | No significant handedness |
| T6 | F | 16 | 16 | 0.00004 | Right-handed |
| T7 | M | 16 | 15 | 0.00056 | Right-handed |
| T8 | F | 16 | 15 | 0.00045 | Right-handed |
| T9 | F | 16 | 2 | 0.00451 | Left-handed |
| T10 | F | 16 | 15 | 0.00051 | Right-handed |
| T11 | M | 15 | 15 | 0.00006 | Right-handed |
| T12 | F | 17 | 12 | 0.14403 | No significant handedness |
| T13 | F | 18 | 16 | 0.00143 | Right-handed |
| T14 | F | 18 | 8 | 0.81469 | No significant handedness |
| T15 | M | 15 | 14 | 0.00094 | Right-handed |
| T16 | M | 16 | 15 | 0.00045 | Right-handed |
| T17 | F | 16 | 2 | 0.00397 | Left-handed |
| T18 | M | 16 | 9 | 0.80119 | No significant handedness |

Overall, 11 toads were classified as right-handed, 3 as left-handed,
and 4 showed no significant handedness. A nonsignificant result does
not establish that a toad has no hand preference.

### 2. Population handedness

I excluded the four toads without significant handedness and tested
whether the remaining 14 toads were equally likely to be right- or
left-handed.

Under the null hypothesis, I simulated the number of right-handed
toads using a probability of 0.5. The two-sided simulated p-value
was 0.05802.

Because p > 0.05, I fail to reject the null hypothesis. Although more
toads were classified as right-handed, this test does not provide
sufficient evidence of unequal right- and left-handedness at the
0.05 significance level.

### 3. Male versus female right-handedness

Six of nine males were classified as right-handed, compared with
five of nine females.

- Male proportion: 0.6667
- Female proportion: 0.5556
- Male minus female difference: 0.1111

I randomized right-handed status among all 18 toads 10,000 times,
keeping sex labels fixed. Toads without significant handedness
were included as not classified right-handed.

The two-sided randomization p-value was 1. Every randomization
produced an absolute difference at least as large as the small
observed difference.

I fail to reject the null hypothesis. These data do not provide
evidence that males and females differ in their probability of
being classified as right-handed.

## Final Problem 2: Assortative Mating in Animals

### 1. Function for mean correlation

My mean_r() function takes a taxon name, selects its correlation
values, and returns their mean.

The mean correlation for Fish was 0.3971901. This positive value
indicates assortative mating by size on average.

### 2. Is the mean especially high for fish?

I sampled 37 correlation values without replacement from the entire
dataset and calculated their mean. I repeated this 50,000 times.

I estimated an upper-tail p-value using the proportion of random
means at least as large as the observed fish mean.

The p-value was 0.00316. At the 0.05 significance level, fish had an
unusually high mean correlation compared with random groups of
37 observations from the full dataset.

### 3. Results for all taxa

For each taxon, I repeated the procedure using its own sample size
and 50,000 random samples.

| Taxon | N | Mean r | Upper-tail p-value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Amphibian | 143 | 0.1855282 | 0.99998 |
| Annelids | 1 | 0.2000000 | 0.59226 |
| Bird | 52 | 0.1317567 | 0.99996 |
| Chelicerate | 9 | 0.4911353 | 0.01068 |
| Crustacean | 149 | 0.4030283 | 0 observed exceedances |
| Fish | 37 | 0.3971901 | 0.00316 |
| Gastropod | 10 | 0.4010000 | 0.07746 |
| Insect | 106 | 0.1966453 | 0.99840 |
| Mammal | 1 | 0.0090000 | 0.84158 |
| Protist | 5 | 0.6140200 | 0.00368 |
| Reptile | 8 | 0.1175000 | 0.93080 |

Chelicerates, crustaceans, fish, and protists had unusually high
mean correlations at an unadjusted significance level of 0.05.

For crustaceans, none of the 50,000 random samples reached the
observed mean. The simulation estimate was zero, which does not
mean the true probability is zero. A plus-one estimate is
1 / 50,001, approximately 0.00002.

The other taxa did not have significantly high means in these
upper-tail tests. These comparisons were not adjusted for
multiple testing.
